  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nANOMALOUS MOISTURE AND INCREASING PRECIP CHANCES THROUGH\nMIDWEEK\n\nThe remnants of TS Rachel continues to influence the Western\nSlope and eastern Utah forecast area as an unusually moist\nairmass remains in place ahead of an upper-level trough digging\ninto the Intermountain West. A blocking ridge downstream is\nslowing the progression of this system and will allow the \ntrough to linger across the region through early Tuesday. Broad \nsouthwest flow aloft and at the surface will keep scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms in the forecast. Cloud cover \nwill inhibit convection today but a quick break in this cloud\ncover should allow some storms to get going. Outside of\nconvection, we are already seeing a line of heavier showers \nmoving from south to north just N of the I-70 corridor bringing\na more soaking type rain. Latest HREF guidance is showing low \nand mid clouds remaining predominant through tomorrow afternoon \nand beyond. Having said all that, the chance still exists for \nconvection so keep an eye to the skies if recreating over the \nnext few days. Some gusty winds of 25 to 30 mph are also \nexpected though not as strong as yesterday. \n\nThe upper low is forecast to dig southeastward, reaching the \nFour Corners by Wednesday morning. As this occurs, a strong jet \nstreak will remain overhead providing wide scale lift and with\nall the moisture remaining, more widespread precip is expected \nthrough the day Tuesday, continuing through Thursday. All higher\nterrain will see the best chances for this precip with adjacent\nvalleys/foothills also seeing some precip, albeit somewhat \nlower. As the low moves across the CWA, precip amounts and \ncoverage will increase from the San Juans Wed early afternoon \nand shift to portions of the central mountains by Wednesday \nevening. Of course, the track of the low is very important on \nwhere the heaviest precip will fall and confidence in this \nregard remains in the medium category.\n\nWith the arrival of colder air, snow levels will drop to 10K\nfeet, if not a little below. This will allow some accumulating\nsnow for the higher terrain, especially above 10K feet. Look \nfor 1 to 2 inches of snow with some locally higher amounts \npossible, especially for the eastern Uintas. Will NOT be issuing\nany highlights as impacts are expected to remain above pass \nlevel and the snow that does fall at that level should melt. \nCan re- evaluate as the colder air moves in. \n\nWraparound moisture from the closed low will finally end\nThursday morning as the parent low moves to the Plains. \nTemperatures will slowly start to increase but will remain at or\njust below normal. Morning lows Thursday could drop below \nfreezing for some northern valleys. A drier pattern develops \nThursday bringing a return to more sunny skies, warming \ntemperatures and dry conditions.",
